[FINAL-TEXT] All evidence is in. Pings fully work, switch STP states are normal (one blocked uplink per access switch as expected for redundancy), dswa1/dswa2 all forwarding. No network fault exists.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Thorough investigation of every network element between PC1 and PC2 found no fault. PC1 (10.1.0.100 on aswa2) and PC2 (10.1.0.101 on aswa3) both have correct IP addresses on the same /24 subnet (10.1.0.0/24), and direct ICMP tests in both directions showed 3/3 packets delivered with zero loss and sub-5 ms round-trip times. The L2 path between them traverses aswa2 → dswa1 → aswa3; Spanning Tree Protocol has correctly placed one redundant uplink on each access switch (aswa2.eth2 and aswa3.eth2, the uplinks toward dswa2) into blocking state, while the primary uplinks (eth1 on each, connecting to dswa1) are forwarding — this is normal dual-uplink STP behavior, not a fault. Distribution switches dswa1 and dswa2 show all ports forwarding. No misconfiguration, downed interface, VLAN mismatch, or STP loop condition was detected anywhere in the path. The user's report of "stopped speaking" does not correspond to any current network-layer failure; connectivity at IP and Ethernet level is fully operational. The root cause is that there is no network anomaly — the issue is either an application-layer or host-side problem (firewall rule, stopped service, credentials, or a transient condition that has since self-resolved) rather than a switch or cabling fault. No network corrective action is required; the helpdesk should redirect the user to check the file-sharing service or host firewall on PC2.
=== END ===